hadiths[10]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1766","Imran bin Husain (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ captured a man from Banu Uqayl, while the people of Thaqif had captured two men from among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ exchanged that man for the two men whom the people of Thaqif had captured."
"1767","Imran bin Husain (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ captured a man from Banu Aqil, so they tied him with ropes and threw him in Harrah (the black stony land of Madinah). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by him and we were with him, or he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him riding a donkey and there was a blanket under him. He called out to you ﷺ saying, ""O Muhammad! O Muhammad!"" So the Prophet ﷺ came to him and asked, ""What is the matter?"" He said, ""For what crime have I been arrested? And for what fault has the she-camel (Adhba), which leads the pilgrims, been seized?"" He ﷺ replied, ""You have been arrested because of the actions of your allies, Thaqif."" And Thaqif had captured two companions of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ left him and went away. Then he called out again, ""O Muhammad! O Muhammad!"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ felt compassion for him and returned to him and asked, ""What is the matter?"" He said, ""I am a Muslim."" The Prophet ﷺ said, ""If you had said this when you were the master of your own affair (i.e., not captured), you would have been completely successful."" Then the Prophet ﷺ left him and went away. He called out again, ""O Muhammad! O Muhammad!"" The Prophet ﷺ returned to him again and he said, ""I am hungry, feed me."" The narrator says, I think he also said, ""And I am thirsty, give me water."" The Prophet ﷺ said, ""This is your need."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ released him in exchange for the two men whom the people of Thaqif had captured, and he also took back his she-camel."
"1768","It is narrated from Imran bin Husain (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) gave one man in exchange for two men."
"1769","It is narrated from Umar bin Abdul Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Prophet ﷺ wrote to the dhimmis of (Yemen) that each person among you is to give one dinar every year, or its equivalent value in Ma'afir (cloth)."
"1770","Mutarrif bin Mazan and Hisham bin Yusuf informed me; they narrated it as a musnad (connected) narration, but I could not retain it, however, its chain is hasan (good): that the Prophet ﷺ imposed one dinar (as payment of jizya) every year on the dhimmis of Yemen. I asked Mutarrif bin Mazan: ""Is it said that (jizya) is also (obligatory) upon women?"" So Mutarrif said that it is not established with them that the Prophet ﷺ took jizya from women."
"1771","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah that the Prophet ﷺ imposed an annual jizya of one dinar on Wahib the Christian in Makkah, and the Prophet ﷺ imposed an annual jizya of 300 dinars on the Christians of Aylah, and (he commanded them) that any Muslim who passed by them should be hosted for three days, and that they should not deceive any Muslim."
"1772","Ishaq bin Abdullah narrated that they (the Christians of Aylah) were 300 in number at that time, so the Prophet ﷺ fixed 300 dinars annually upon them."
"1773","Ja'far bin Muhammad narrated from his father that Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) mentioned the Magians and said: ""I do not know what to do regarding their matter."" So Abdur Rahman bin Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: Treat them in the same way as you treat the People of the Book."""
"1774","Bajalah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) did not take jizyah from the Magians until Abdur Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) testified that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had taken jizyah from the Magians of Hajar."
"1775","Nasr bin Asim narrated that Farwah bin Nawfal Ashja'i said: ""Why is jizyah taken from the Magians? They are not People of the Book."" (Upon hearing this) Mustawrid stood up towards him, grabbed him by the collar, and said: ""O enemy of Allah! Are you now criticizing Abu Bakr, Umar, and the Commander of the Faithful (i.e., Ali, may Allah be pleased with them), while they took jizyah from them (the Magians)?"" Then he took him towards the palace (of the caliphate), and Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came out and said: ""Both of you, be gentle."" So they both sat in the shade of the palace. Then Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I am the most knowledgeable among the people regarding the Magians. They had knowledge which they knew, and a book which they used to read. (It happened that) their king became drunk and committed adultery with his daughter or his sister. Some of the prominent people of his kingdom found out about this. When he woke up and the people came to carry out the prescribed punishment on him, he stopped them and called the prominent people of his kingdom and said: Do you know of any religion better than the religion of Adam (peace be upon him)? Adam (peace be upon him) used to marry his sons to his daughters, and I am (also) upon the religion of Adam (peace be upon him). Why do you distance yourselves from his religion? Then those people followed him and fought against their opponents until they killed them. Then their situation became such that guards were placed over their book. Then it was taken away from them, and knowledge was removed from their hearts, and they are among the People of the Book. And from them, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) took jizyah."""
